    <relatedmaterial id="aspace_900084c22a9c9d39f7440fdcc5bad58d">
      <head>Related Materials</head>
      <p>Related collections include the organizational records of Save the Pine Bush, Inc. (apap125), the Louis Ismay papers (ua902-064), the Environmental Studies Program records (ua696-003), the Concerned Citizens Against Crossgates records (apap057), and the Crossgates Regional Shopping Mall collection (apap067). See the subject guide on Conservation and the Environment for further collections relating to environmentalism in the Capital Region and beyond.</p>
    </relatedmaterial>
    <scopecontent id="aspace_e556ea55ac684db9d29f0d1aacc11e50">
      <head>Scope and Contents</head>
      <p>The collection documents Wolcott's involvement with the Pinxter Committee of Albany and Save the Pine Bush, Inc., as well as his general work in historical research, archaeology, and environmental preservation within the Capital Region. Materials include event posters, flyers, and programs; research notes and materials; news clippings; correspondence, particularly with local legislators; photographs; and Wolcott's notebooks and notepads. Material of note relating to Pinxter Fest include an early souvenir Tulip Fest envelope, contact information for craft vendors, and a small collection of colonial Dutch recipes. Material of note relating to the Pine Bush include archaeological reports, petitions, maps, and trail guides. Maps are attributed to the creators when that information is known. There are instances where Wolcott edited existing maps originally created by others. If the date of the edits is unknown, the date of the original map is given.</p>
      <p>The collection is particularly strong in its documentation of ad-hoc and grassroots advocacy on behalf of the Pine Bush during the 1970s; Wolcott's dealings with the City of Albany, the Town of Guilderland, and the Town of Colonie; and Wolcott's research into the following local history topics: the Maquas Padt/King's Highway, the Isaac Truax tavern, Henry Rowe Schoolcraft, and the changes in land ownership within the Albany Pine Bush across time. Some files from the early 1970s were listed as having belonged to Wolcott's friend and colleague, George Keleshian, and these are noted in the folder titles. Many of Wolcott's personal papers documenting his research into the colonial Dutch heritage of the city of Albany were lost in a 2021 house fire. </p>
    </scopecontent>
